WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
Linux security fundamentals become useful when they become a repeatable operating routine.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.580
Start with inventory.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.400
Know which systems exist, who owns them, what purpose they serve, what data they handle,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.660
which users and service accounts depend on them, and

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.040
what recovery expectations apply.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.660
A system without an owner is difficult to secure because nobody can approve risk,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.600
validate changes, or accept exceptions.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.700
Then reduce unnecessary privilege and exposure.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:35.080
Review users, groups, sudo access, root practices, service accounts, file owners,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.300
file modes, services, daemons, processes, and open ports.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.520
The goal is not to remove everything.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.580
The goal is to match access and behavior to the system's purpose.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.120
Least privilege should apply to people, services, files, and network reachability.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.100
Maintain trusted software.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.360
Use approved repositories, track packages, remove unused components carefully,

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:02.380
and apply patches through a process that includes testing, backups, change windows,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.200
and rollback planning.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.980
Secure remote access with controlled paths, justified SSH exposure,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.000
documented firewall rules, and monitoring.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:16.140
Enable logs, protect them, retain them long enough to be useful,

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:21.240
and review high-value signals such as authentication, sudo, service changes,

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.140
and package activity.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.360
Finally, keep improving.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:31.180
Test restores, validate hardening changes, document assumptions, review exceptions,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.200
and adjust baselines as systems change.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.980
Linux security is not one command, one scan, or one checklist.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.320
It is disciplined operation across identity, configuration, software, network exposure,

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.180
monitoring, backups, and recovery.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.040
Practical habits repeated consistently are what make Linux systems safer and more

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.220
reliable.
